+A looped language model applies the same block of layers $T$ times at
+each token position. We call one application of the block a \emph{loop
+step}. One step can advance several positions by one loop each, even
+when the positions have completed different numbers of loops. Greedy
+decoding waits until the current position finishes all $T$ loops before
+starting the next position. LoopFill instead proposes upcoming tokens,
+called \emph{drafts}, using earlier text and the model's own intermediate
+predictions. The decoder can start processing a proposed token before
+verifying it. It appends a token to the output only when that token is
+the greedy next-token prediction after all $T$ loops at the last
+verified position.
 
